{"records":[{"id":"mk7z7st438","title":"ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition. Rainey et al","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is required for activation of licensed origins of replication during the process of DNA duplication. CDC7 kinase inhibitors interfere with this process, limiting both DNA synthesis and cell proliferation.\n\nAim:\nIdentify genes responsible for limiting cell proliferation in response to inhibition of CDC7 using small molecule ATP-competitive inhibitors.\n\nStrategy:\nA CRISPR/Cas9 loss of function screen was performed to identify genes that upon loss allow bypass of a proliferation blockade imposed by the CDC7 inhibitor, XL413.\n\nData:\nRepresents source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the Manuscript 'ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition', Rainey et al.\n\nData collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-19T20:26:38.851Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mk7z7st438","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mk7z7st438"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Rainey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Replication","Cell Signaling","Genetic Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m9d29fhmc3","title":"METABOLOMIC DATA OF EGFR-TKI-SENSITIVE CELLS VS. EGFR-TKI-RESISTANCE CELLS","description":"Epider growth factor receptor-tyrosine kinase inhibitor (EGFR-TKI) acquired-resistance remains unknown. We determined a metabolomics profiling comparing EGFR-TKI-resistant cells to EGFR-TKI-sensitive cells using UPLC-QTOF-MS.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-12T10:08:35.682Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m9d29fhmc3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m9d29fhmc3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yu-Fei","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":"0000-0003-2475-570X"},{"first_name":"Ke-Ren","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ya-Bin","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"54xr9bsxd2","title":"Metabolomic data of H460 and A549 cells after trametinib treatment","description":"MAP kinse-ERK kinase inhibitor (MEKi) resistance remains unknown. We determined gene expression profiling in MEKi-resistant cells and MEKi-sensitive cells after MEKi treatment using UHPLC-MS/MS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-01T11:53:52.553Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54xr9bsxd2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54xr9bsxd2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhengke","last_name":"lian","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology","Metabolomics","Drug Resistance"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v4fk8pbnbm","title":"LINF_320020300","description":"ATR kinase\nLeishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-07T11:15:05.951Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v4fk8pbnbm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v4fk8pbnbm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hr65t4zzkv","title":"A comprehensive human gastric cancer organoid biobank captures tumor subtype heterogeneity and enables therapeutic screening. Yan HHN et al. Mendeley Dataset 17","description":"Dose response of gastric cancer organoids with and without ARD1A mutation to treatment with ATR inhibitor VE-821","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:23:24.471Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hr65t4zzkv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hr65t4zzkv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Helen HN","last_name":"Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suet Yi","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stomach Cancer","Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s83f5y9ntt","title":"DUB3 promotes BET inhibitor resistance and cancer progression through deubiquitinating BRD4. Jin et al","description":"The bromodomain and extra-terminal domain (BET) protein BRD4 is emerging as a promising anticancer therapeutic target. However, resistance to BET inhibitors often occurs, and it has been linked to aberrant degradation of BRD4 protein in cancer. Here, we demonstrate that the deubiquitinase DUB3 binds to BRD4 and promotes its deubiquitination and stabilization. Expression of DUB3 is transcriptionally repressed by the NCOR2-HDAC10 complex. The NCOR2 gene is frequently deleted in castration-resistant prostate cancer patient specimens, and loss of NCOR2 induces elevation of DUB3 and BRD4 proteins in cancer cells. DUB3-proficient prostate cancer cells are resistant to the BET inhibitor JQ1 in vitro and in mice, but this effect is diminished by DUB3 inhibitory agents such as CDK4/6 inhibitor in a RB-independent manner. Our findings identify a previously unrecognized mechanism causing BRD4 upregulation and drug resistance, suggesting that DUB3 is a viable therapeutic target to overcome BET inhibitor resistance in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-13T19:55:44.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s83f5y9ntt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s83f5y9ntt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunoprecipitation","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"7rgn4p4cvp","title":"PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is crucial for the initiation of DNA replication and is important for replication stress responses. Several ATP-competitive CDC7 inhibitors (CDC7is) are being developed as possible cancer therapeutics. CDC7 inhibition causes a dose-dependent delay in S-phase progression and mild activation of the S-phase checkpoint pathway, which further limits origin firing through ATR activation.\n\nMethodology:\nTo identify genes that modulate the cellular responses to CDC7is, we established a CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide loss-of-function screen. The following data represent the closer investigation of one of these genes, PTBP1, using RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, immunofluorescence analysis and additional techniques as described in the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors'.\n\nData:\nData represent source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors', Göder et al. Data collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:30:16.52Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7rgn4p4cvp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7rgn4p4cvp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anja","last_name":"Göder","orcid":"0000-0001-9743-1656"},{"first_name":"Corrado","last_name":"Santocanale","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/501100001602","name":"Science Foundation Ireland","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","Cell Cycle","Cell Cycle Checkpoint","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5gdzg3vthj","title":"Convection-Enhanced Delivery of a Macrocyclic ATR Inhibitor Synergizes with Systemic Lomustine in Preclinical Glioblastoma Models","description":"Source image files for the research article \"Convection-Enhanced Delivery of a Macrocyclic ATR Inhibitor Synergizes with Systemic Lomustine in Preclinical Glioblastoma Models\" by Josowitz et al, 2026.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-13T17:55:27.889Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5gdzg3vthj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5gdzg3vthj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Repair","Drug Delivery","Nanoparticle","Glioblastoma Multiforme","DNA Damage Response","Combination Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4rk3nntzbs","title":"Bromodomain and HDAC inhibitors equivalently overcome PI3K/AKT inhibition resistance in cancers","description":"Blocking BRD4 or HDACs induces paradoxically similar gene expression, comparably attenuates PI3K/AKT inhibitor resistance in multiple cancer cells. PI3K/AKT inhibitors induce global loss of histone acetylation. While in the resistant cells, specific genome loci gain H3K27 acetylation and BRD4 binding coupled with up-regulation of selective genes. These loci and genes are vulnerable to BRD4 or CBP/p300 bromodomain inhibitors, as well as HDAC inhibitors. Inadequate occupancy of HDACs on these loci permits gain of H3K27 acetylation and BRD4 recruitment after AKT inhibition. Bromodomain inhibitors directly suppress these loci; HDAC inhibitors induce histone hyper-acetylation outside these loci, offering competitive BRD4 binding sites, re-localizing BRD4. To suppress these loci, genes, and relative resistance, multi-bromodomain inhibitor NEO2734 and HDAC inhibitors are equivalent in effects. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-27T20:42:13.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4rk3nntzbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4rk3nntzbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"DI","last_name":"WU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Histone Deacetylase","Gene Transcription","Histone Code"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6xnbgvjvx9","title":"Supplemental Material - Investigating Barriers to Systemic Janus Kinase Inhibitor Access for Alopecia Areata","description":"Supplemental tables for the publication entitled, \"Investigating Barriers to Systemic Janus Kinase Inhibitor Access for Alopecia Areata.\" ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:21:27.714Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6xnbgvjvx9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6xnbgvjvx9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carli","last_name":"Needle","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Brinks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nnaemeka","last_name":"Anyanwu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caitlin","last_name":"Kearney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ambika","last_name":"Nohria","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Klein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dolly","last_name":"Taiwò","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Efe","last_name":"Kakpovbia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Laughter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jerry","last_name":"Shapiro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kristen","last_name":"Lo Sicco","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Disparity","Alopecia","Alopecia Areata"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":79433,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":94,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":79433,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":79304,"restricted":42,"embargoed":87},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1322},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","c